      "source_sha256": "52e82e1152291bdb04feb6aaa7e7d1f021a7a77149a35ec3ba1d4c781ba97b68",
      "rationale_from_source": "KMNIST: beat a single ResNet-9 reference (0.45% dev error) by training a diverse\n*ensemble* of ResNet-9 style CNNs with per-sample GPU affine augmentation and\naveraging softmax over a small no-flip test-time-augmentation set.\n\nRationale (exploratory, unverified before measurement):\n * The reference augments a whole batch with ONE shared crop offset and no\n   rotation/scale/shear.  KMNIST characters vary in stroke placement and slant;\n   per-sample random affine (rotation +-12deg, shear, scale, translation) plus\n   random erasing is a much stronger regulariser for only 12k training rows.\n * At 0.45% error the remaining mistakes are dominated by variance, not bias.\n   Averaging independently seeded/width-varied networks plus mild TTA is the\n   cheapest reliable variance reduction, and it applies identically on the audit\n   split (no transduction, no dev pseudo-labels, so dev gains should transfer).\n * Horizontal flips are NOT used anywhere (train or TTA), per the task contract.\n * Time is budgeted adaptively: each member gets an equal slice of the remaining\n   training budget and its LR schedule advances by max(step fraction, time\n   fraction) so every member always completes a full cosine schedule.\nLimitations: no held-out internal validation (all 12k labelled rows are used by\nevery member to maximise accuracy at this error level), so the findings report\ntraining-set diagnostics only; the ensemble size can shrink if the shared GPU is\nslow.",
